(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which is the MOST COMMON malignant nail tumour, and what is it associated with?","choices":["Nail unit squamous cell carcinoma or Bowen disease, associated with high-risk human papillomavirus, immunosuppression, chronic inflammation or trauma, prior radiation, and older age","Nail unit melanoma, associated with high-risk human papillomavirus, immunosuppression, chronic inflammation or trauma, prior radiation, and older age, and often multifocal","Nail unit basal cell carcinoma, associated with cumulative ultraviolet exposure, fair skin, chronic trauma, prior radiation and older age","Glomus tumour, associated with chronic inflammation or trauma, immunosuppression, prior radiation and older age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — periungual disease may be multifocal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which topical regimens does the deck give for selected superficial basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Vismodegib or sonidegib taken orally for 6 to 10 weeks, with clinical clearance confirmed afterwards","Liquid nitrogen cryotherapy every two to three weeks for 6 to 10 weeks, with clearance confirmed afterwards","Imiquimod five nights weekly for 6 to 10 weeks, or fluorouracil twice daily for up to 12 weeks, with clinical clearance confirmed afterwards","Imiquimod twice daily for 6 to 10 weeks, or fluorouracil five nights weekly for up to 12 weeks, with clearance assumed once inflammation settles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — confirming clearance is part of the regimen. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which features are NOT part of a typical actinic keratosis and should prompt biopsy consideration?","choices":["Tenderness on palpation and a rough surface texture","Flesh-coloured or pink colour and a size under a centimetre","Location on the dorsal hand and the presence of surrounding sun damage","Bleeding, induration, ulceration or rapid enlargement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — none of these belong to a typical lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which indications does the deck give for Mohs micrographic surgery in squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Any lesion on the trunk, any lesion in a patient over 65, any lesion present more than a year, any pigmented lesion, and any lesion the patient finds unsightly","Only recurrent tumours and only after two failed courses of topical therapy, since site, size and immune status do not alter the surgical choice","High-risk sites (lips, temples, ears, nose, genitalia), recurrent tumours, aggressive histology with perineural or perivascular invasion, lesions over 1 cm on the face or over 2 cm on trunk or extremities, immunosuppression, tumours within scars, and genetic disease-associated tumours","High-risk sites (lips, temples, ears, nose, genitalia), recurrent tumours, aggressive histology with perineural or perivascular invasion, lesions over 2 cm on the face or over 1 cm on trunk or extremities, immunosuppression, and tumours within scars"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — note the two different size thresholds by site. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which melanoma subtype does the deck say is frequently delayed in diagnosis, making early reporting critical?","choices":["Superficial spreading melanoma, the commonest subtype overall","Lentigo maligna melanoma","Nodular melanoma","Acral lentiginous melanoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why patients are told to report new pigment, widening bands or periungual pigment promptly. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which TNM combination defines stage 0 melanoma?","choices":["T1a, N0, M0","Tis, N1a, M0","T0, N0, M1","Tis, N0, M0"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ with no nodal or distant disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What proportion of patients develop a second basal cell carcinoma, and what does that mandate?","choices":["Up to 50%, which makes examination every five years sufficient","Under 1%, which makes routine surveillance unnecessary after excision","Up to 50%, which makes at least annual full-skin examination mandatory","Up to 5%, which makes examination every five years sufficient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the second-primary risk is what drives surveillance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In which three immunosuppressed groups is basal cell carcinoma more common and more likely to recur?","choices":["Pregnancy, and long-term oral contraceptive or corticosteroid use","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma, solid-organ transplant, and allogeneic stem cell transplant","Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, human immunodeficiency virus, and organ transplantation","Diabetes, chronic kidney disease and cirrhosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three groups named on the risk factor slide. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What must be assessed when planning basal cell carcinoma treatment?","choices":["Whether the patient can tolerate hedgehog inhibitors","Whether it is low-risk versus morpheaform, micronodular, infiltrative, recurrent, or in a tissue-sensitive site","Whether the patient has previously taken oral nicotinamide as chemoprevention for a prior lesion","Whether the lesion is pigmented or not"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck says this directly changes treatment selection. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which local reactions should an actinic keratosis patient be warned to expect from field therapy?","choices":["Permanent hypopigmentation at every treated site, which is expected and harmless","Blistering that requires the course to be abandoned","No visible reaction at all if the therapy is working","Erythema and crusting, alongside adherence to the full treatment duration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — patients who are not warned stop the course early. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which cutaneous T-cell lymphoma features worsen prognosis, and which carries the best?","choices":["Prognosis is uniformly poor regardless of stage","Tumours, erythroderma and lymphadenopathy worsen it; limited patch disease does not reduce survival","Patch and plaque disease worsen it; tumour-stage disease carries the better prognosis","Only lymphadenopathy worsens it; skin findings do not affect prognosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — survival is not reduced in limited patch-stage disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What defines stage IV melanoma in the staging overview?","choices":["Any T with N0 and M0 — a thick primary with no nodal or distant disease","Tis with any N and any M — in situ disease with any degree of spread","Any T, any N, with M1 — that is, distant metastasis present whatever the primary or nodal status","T4b, N3a/b/c, with M0 — the deepest primary with extensive nodal disease but no distant spread"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — M1 alone determines it. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A course of field therapy has cleared a patient's actinic keratoses. What must not be assumed?","choices":["That surveillance can stop, because the surrounding field remains at risk","That the treatment reactions of erythema and crusting were abnormal and should not recur","That daily sun protection is still required","That a nonhealing lesion should be reviewed promptly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — ongoing surveillance remains necessary despite successful therapy. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which factors does the deck list as amplifying actinic keratosis risk?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis, asthma and allergic rhinitis in a young adult, with a family history of atopy and frequent emollient use","Advanced age, cumulative ultraviolet exposure with outdoor work or recreation, prior actinic keratosis or keratinocyte carcinoma, male sex with bald scalp exposure, and immunosuppression","Young age, intermittent ultraviolet exposure with indoor occupation, no prior actinic keratosis or keratinocyte carcinoma, female sex with full scalp hair, and a fully competent immune system","Obesity, diabetes with vascular disease, incontinence and immobility in a hospitalised patient, and recent antibiotic exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — five amplifiers. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How often should a patient treated for squamous cell carcinoma be examined, and how does immunosuppression change it?","choices":["At least annual skin and lymph-node examination, with closer intervals if high-risk or immunosuppressed","At least an annual skin examination alone, at an interval unchanged by immunosuppression","Every three months for life in all patients","Only when the patient reports a new lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the interval tightens rather than the examination changing. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which history elements does the deck ask you to elicit?","choices":["Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational or recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; prior skin cancer, immunosuppression or transplant, human immunodeficiency virus risk; chronic scars, wounds or radiation sites; family history","Onset and change, bleeding or non-healing, pain or pruritus; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars, wounds and radiation sites; family history — but not prior skin cancer, immunosuppression, transplant or human immunodeficiency virus status, which the dermatologist obtains at the time of biopsy","Onset and change; sunburns, occupational exposure and tanning-bed use; chronic scars and radiation sites; family history — but not immunosuppression or transplant status, which do not alter cutaneous malignancy risk","Onset and change, pain or pruritus; occupational and recreational ultraviolet exposure and tanning-bed use; immunosuppression and family history — but not prior skin cancer, which does not predict a second primary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — note chronic scars and radiation sites among them. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the classic presentation of cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Red or purple macules, plaques and nodules on skin or mucous membranes; also marked edema with few visible lesions","A small red, conical, hard nodule that may ulcerate; also a non-healing ulcer, a warty nodule, or an irregular pink plaque with haemorrhagic crust","A pearly translucent papule with visible telangiectasias and a rolled border; also a scar-like ivory-white plaque with subtle extension","An asymmetric pigmented macule with irregular borders and colour variegation; also an amelanotic nodule lacking classic features"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — four presentations to recognise. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A single nail abnormality has persisted despite treatment. What does the deck say to avoid?","choices":["Repeated empiric antifungal, antibiotic or wart treatment","Photographing the concerning nail before any referral is made","Examining the other nine nails","Removing the nail polish before examination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — repeating empiric therapy on a solitary persistent nail lesion is how diagnostic delay happens. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What harm does the deck attribute to overly aggressive cutaneous T-cell lymphoma therapy?","choices":["Complications and premature death","Accelerated transformation into a systemic non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Permanent phototherapy intolerance","Loss of eligibility for a cutaneous lymphoma centre"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and early aggressive treatment has not been proven to cure disease or prevent progression. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which team must be involved BEFORE immunosuppression is reduced in iatrogenic Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Pulmonology or gastroenterology","The transplant team","Medical oncology, who direct all systemic and immunosuppressive decisions","Palliative care"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck lists them explicitly as a referral before reducing immunosuppression. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which variable does the deck call the dominant prognostic factor in melanoma, and what modifies it?","choices":["Patient age, with ulceration and mitotic activity further modifying stage-based prognosis","The anatomic site of the primary, with ulceration and mitotic activity further modifying prognosis","Breslow thickness, which must be accurately measured at the time of initial biopsy; ulceration and mitotic activity further modify stage-based prognosis","Lesion diameter, which must be accurately measured at the time of initial biopsy; ulceration and mitotic activity further modify prognosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why the initial biopsy technique matters. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What must a patient starting antiretroviral therapy for Kaposi sarcoma be told?","choices":["That the lesions may worsen early, as immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome","That the lesions will always begin improving within the first two weeks of therapy","That antiretroviral therapy should be stopped if lesions enlarge","That oral lesions never respond to therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — warning them beforehand stops early worsening being read as treatment failure. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which Kaposi sarcoma form may REGRESS, and what makes it do so?","choices":["Classic disease, which regresses spontaneously after a course of local radiation","Endemic disease, which regresses with antiretroviral therapy","Epidemic disease, which regresses once chemotherapy stops","Iatrogenic disease, which may regress when immunosuppression is reduced"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — which is why the transplant team is consulted before any reduction. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the referral threshold for melanoma to an expert centre?","choices":["Only melanoma that has already failed immunotherapy","Melanoma deeper than 1 mm, or with lymph-node or other-site spread","Melanoma deeper than 4 mm only, since thinner disease is managed entirely in primary care","Any melanoma in a patient over 65, regardless of thickness or spread"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — confirmed melanoma warrants coordinated dermatology and surgical oncology care. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the classic triad of a glomus tumour?","choices":["A rapidly growing friable bleeding papule with an epithelial collarette","A small red-blue subungual focus with severe paroxysmal pain, exquisite point tenderness, and cold sensitivity","A verrucous periungual plaque with subungual hyperkeratosis, onycholysis and painless bleeding","A longitudinal pigmented band with proximal widening, blurred borders and nail splitting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the nail may look nearly normal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which red flags does the deck list for squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Slow growth, painlessness, a stable size, soft consistency, free mobility, and no palpable nodes","Pruritus, fine scaling, symmetry of the lesion, sharp demarcation, and a waxy stuck-on surface","Sandpaper texture, tenderness on palpation, flesh-coloured hue, and a size under 6 mm","Rapid growth, pain, bleeding, ulceration, induration, fixation, or palpable regional nodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — seven features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does Clark level differ from Breslow thickness, and which does the deck call the dominant prognostic variable?","choices":["Both describe the same measurement on different scales, and either may be used as the dominant prognostic variable","Clark level is the anatomic layer reached; Breslow thickness is a measured depth, and Clark is the dominant prognostic variable","Clark level is the anatomic layer reached; Breslow thickness is a measured depth, and Breslow is the dominant prognostic variable","Clark level is a measured depth; Breslow thickness is the anatomic layer reached, and Clark is the dominant prognostic variable"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — Breslow is measured in millimetres and drives margins and staging. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the core mechanism of actinic keratosis, and how does the deck position it relative to keratinocyte carcinoma?","choices":["Human papillomavirus infection of keratinocytes in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is a benign lesion lying on a continuum with verruca rather than with keratinocyte carcinoma","Chronic thermal injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of scarred skin; it is premalignant and lies on a continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma but is unrelated to ultraviolet exposure","Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma, not a distinct separate entity","Chronic ultraviolet injury producing dysplastic melanocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin; it is premalignant but a distinct entity quite separate from keratinocyte carcinoma, with no biologic continuum between them"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a continuum, not a separate disease. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In which patients is Kaposi sarcoma immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome especially dangerous?","choices":["Those with classic Kaposi sarcoma, which is indolent","Those with only oral lesions","Those over seventy years old","Those with visceral disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — endemic or visceral disease and this syndrome can be aggressive and rapidly fatal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which initial skin-directed therapies does the deck list for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids, topical mechlorethamine, bexarotene gel, and ultraviolet phototherapy","Topical corticosteroids, topical fluorouracil, imiquimod, and photodynamic therapy","Intralesional vincristine, vinblastine, bleomycin, and radiation","Liposomal doxorubicin, paclitaxel, romidepsin and vorinostat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — four skin-directed options. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"}]};
